question_text:
At the New Year's Day party, a total of $$100$$ people participated in three types of performances: dancing, singing, and playing. It is known that:
(1)$$50$$ people participated in dancing, and $$40$$ people participated in playing;
(2)The number of people who only participate in chorus is twice the number of people who only participate in dancing;
(3)$$15$$ people participated in dancing and chorusing but did not participate in playing;
(4)There are $$6$$ more people who only participate in playing than those who take part in both playing and singing but not dancing.
How many people participated in both playing and singing but not dancing?

source_content_english:
At the New Year's Day party, a total of $$100$$ people participated in three types of performances: dancing, singing, and playing. It is known that:
(1)$$50$$ people participated in dancing, and $$40$$ people participated in playing;
(2)The number of people who only participate in chorus is twice the number of people who only participate in dancing;
(3)$$15$$ people participated in dancing and chorusing but did not participate in playing;
(4)There are $$6$$ more people who only participate in playing than those who take part in both playing and singing but not dancing.
Then there are those who participated in both playing and singing but not dancing.

